<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Valois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1328–1589</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Valois was a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a> that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1328, when Philip VI succeeded the last direct Capetian, until 1589. It led France through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, and its junior lines included the Valois dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. On the death of Henry III in 1589 the throne passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-valois.jpg" alt="House of Valois" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlodangio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Philippe_de_Valois.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
